Chapter 12
Something lit up inside Curtis.
It started behind the mask—both eyes flaring so hard the slits couldn’t contain it. Red and blue lightning cracked outward from the sockets. Green lines ignited across the damaged armor, racing along the scaled plates, sealing cracks, rebuilding shattered sections in real time. The tattered cloak snapped like it had weight again. Antlers hummed. The whole suit locked back into place, brighter and heavier than before.
He rolled his neck. The sound was clean.
“Okay. Fine then.” His voice came lower, edged with something new. “Let’s amp this up.”
Nomu charged. Curtis met it. The collision this time didn’t stagger him. Green light flared at the point of impact. He drove the monster back a full step, boots grinding, and didn’t give the ground he’d just taken. Shigaraki’s next Decay swipe met a forearm that refused to crumble. The fingers slid off reinforced plating.
Curtis planted and swung. The punch carried the green current with it. Nomu’s head snapped sideways. Regeneration started—and slowed.
On the stairs Aizawa’s eye widened. Midoriya’s breath caught. The plaza lights flickered under the new output.
Curtis set his feet in the center of the cracked ground, armor glowing, lightning still dancing off the mask, and raised his fists again.
“Come on.”
Shigaraki lunged, all five fingers extended for Curtis’s chest.
Curtis caught the wrist mid-reach. Decay flared on contact—black-red erosion racing across the green-lit plates. It ate an inch. Then it stopped. The ancient cryptid material under the armor simply refused it. The erosion died against a surface that had outlasted worse things than quirks.
Curtis’s mask tilted. The smile was audible in his voice.
“What’s wrong? Can’t turn me to dust?” The grip tightened. Green current crawled up Shigaraki’s arm. “Sorry. Your quirk’s nothing new to this armor.”
He yanked the ringleader forward and drove his fist into Shigaraki’s face.
The impact cracked like a gunshot. Shigaraki left the ground, coat whipping, and crashed through a broken section of plaza wall twenty meters back. Dust and debris exploded outward. He didn’t get up immediately.
Nomu roared and charged to fill the gap. Curtis released the limp wrist, rolled his shoulder, and turned to meet the monster with both fists raised, armor still glowing, lightning snapping off the antlers.
“Next.”
The exchange turned brutal.
Nomu’s fists came like pistons—each one heavy enough to crater concrete. Curtis met them. Green-lit armor rang with every block. He slipped inside a wide swing and hammered three shots into the monster’s ribs, lightning crackling on impact. Nomu regenerated through it and answered with an elbow that staggered him. Curtis Blinked clear, reappeared above, and drove both heels into the exposed brain. The dome shook.
They crashed together again in the center of the plaza. Punch for punch. No finesse left. Just output. Nomu’s yellow eyes stayed empty. Curtis’s slits burned. Every collision threw dust and broken tile. The green lines on the armor flared brighter with each hit he landed and dimmed a fraction with each one he took.
On the stairs Aizawa forced the words out between coughs, eye locked on the fight.
“That thing… was made to fight All Might.” Blood hit the step. “Shock absorption. Regeneration. Super strength. It’s not a normal villain. If Beardy keeps matching it blow for blow he’s going to break before it does.”
Midoriya’s hands clenched. Tsuyu’s grip on Aizawa tightened. Minoru had gone silent.
Another thunderous exchange rocked the plaza. Curtis slid back two meters, boots carving trenches, then charged again anyway. Aizawa’s jaw locked.
“He needs to create distance or change the angle. Matching that thing’s strength is exactly what it was built for.”
The fight didn’t slow. Nomu swung. Curtis answered. The dome kept shaking.
Nomu closed the gap faster than the last exchange.
The first punch snapped Curtis’s head hard to the left. The second cracked it back the other way before the mask could settle. Antlers jerked. Green light stuttered across the plates. A third strike—an open-handed blow to the chest—lifted him off his feet.
Nomu didn’t let him land clean. It caught him mid-air and hurled him across the plaza. Curtis smashed through a standing section of ruined wall and kept going, armor sparking, until he cratered into a deeper pile of rubble on the far side. Concrete and rebar collapsed over him. Dust roared outward.
Silence held for one second.
On the stairs Aizawa’s eye widened. Midoriya took a half-step forward before Tsuyu’s tongue stopped him. Minoru flinched at the impact.
The rubble shifted. A green-lit hand pushed a slab aside. Curtis rose from the debris, mask cracked along one antler, lightning still snapping from the eye slits. He rolled his neck until it popped, spat dust, and set his feet again in the wreckage.
Nomu was already walking toward him, regeneration knitting the earlier damage, yellow eyes empty.
Curtis raised his fists. The armor flickered, then steadied.
“Still here.”
The dust hadn’t settled before more of Class 1-A reached the edges of the plaza.
Kirishima and Bakugo skidded to a stop on a broken walkway, both breathing hard. Kirishima’s hardening flickered. “Holy— he’s still going?! That thing just threw him through a wall!”
Bakugo’s palms crackled, eyes locked on the rubble. “Damn extra… get up. Get the hell up.”
Todoroki arrived from the downpour side, half his costume iced over, expression tight. He didn’t speak. He just watched the green-lit figure push free of the debris.
Mina and Jiro came from the ruins zone, supporting each other. Mina’s usual grin was gone. “That’s not a normal villain. Curtis is matching it and it’s still not enough.”
On the stairs Midoriya’s voice shook. “Mr. Aizawa’s right. It’s built for All Might. If he keeps trading punches—”
“He knows,” Tsuyu said quietly, tongue still anchoring their teacher. “Ribbit. He’s still standing anyway.”
Iida’s engines echoed from the upper level—he’d made it out and back with help already coming, but the fight below hadn’t stopped. Uraraka floated near the entrance overlook, hands over her mouth, cheeks pale. Momo stood beside her, already creating a staff she wasn’t sure she’d get to use.
Aizawa coughed again, eye never leaving the plaza. “Don’t interfere. Any of you. That thing will kill you if you step in wrong.”
Below, Curtis rolled his shoulders in the rubble, armor flickering back to full glow, and faced Nomu as the monster closed the distance again. The students watched from every angle they could reach, the dome suddenly full of witnesses, none of them able to look away.
Curtis reached into the inner pocket of the tattered cloak. He pulled a crumpled cigarette and a lighter, tilted the cracked mask just enough, and lit it underneath. The tip glowed. Smoke curled out through the deer-skull’s teeth.
He took a drag, eyes on Nomu, and exhaled slow.
“The more I exchange punches with you, the more likely I lose.” His voice stayed even under the filter. “Shock absorption. Regeneration. Troublesome.” Another short drag. “Oh well. I’ll just have to hit you even harder.”
He flicked the cigarette. It spun through the air and bounced off Nomu’s chest.
Then he amped it.
The green lines on the armor detonated brighter—almost white at the edges. Lightning exploded from both eye slits, red and blue arcing wild across the antlers. The air around him warped. Cracks raced farther through the plaza under his boots. The output jumped so hard the nearest students staggered.
Mina’s mouth fell open. “He can go higher?!”
Kirishima’s hardening flared on instinct. “That’s not— dude, that’s not human!”
Midoriya’s notebook was forgotten in his hand. “The limiter… he just removed more of it—”
Bakugo’s explosions popped uncontrolled. “WHAT THE HELL, EXTRA?!”
Todoroki’s eyes narrowed, ice creeping further up his arm. Uraraka floated an inch off the ground without meaning to. Even Aizawa’s visible eye widened a fraction.
Nomu’s head tilted, regeneration still working, as Curtis rolled his neck, smoke still drifting from the mask, and raised fists that now hummed with the new current.
“Round two.”
The next exchange broke the air.
Curtis and Nomu collided in the center of the plaza and the shockwave tore outward in a visible ring. Students on the walkways staggered. Loose rubble lifted and spun. The dome’s lights flickered hard. Every punch after that carried the same weight—thunderous, ground-splitting, the kind of impact that left afterimages.
Curtis drove a lightning-wrapped fist into Nomu’s jaw. The monster’s head snapped back. It answered with a blow that cratered the floor under Curtis’s boots and sent another shockwave screaming across the plaza. They didn’t separate. Blow for blow. Green light against regeneration. The air itself started to crack.
A sharp sound cut through the noise.
The deer-skull mask fractured. A clean split ran from the left antler down across the red eye slit. Pieces of bone-white plating flaked away. The green lines on the armor stuttered, dimmed, then fought back to full brightness. The suit was reaching its limit.
On the stairs Aizawa’s voice came out hoarse. “The output’s exceeding the costume’s tolerance. He can’t hold that level much longer.”
Midoriya’s hands shook. “If the armor fails while he’s still matching it—”
Another collision. Another shockwave. The crack in the mask widened. Curtis didn’t slow down. He planted, lightning still arcing, and swung again. Nomu met him. The dome shook like it might not survive the next one.
Nomu’s fist crashed through the fractured mask.
The deer skull shattered. Bone-white fragments and antler pieces exploded outward. The red and blue light died with it. Curtis’s face was suddenly bare—braids whipping, heterochromia exposed, blood already welling at his hairline. The follow-up blow drove him straight down. He hit the plaza floor so hard the concrete buckled into a crater around his body.
Nomu didn’t stop. It dropped with him and started raining punches. Each one hammered Curtis deeper into the broken ground. Armor plates cracked and dimmed. Green lines flickered out one by one. The shocks traveled through the dome. Dust and blood misted with every impact.
On the walkways the class reacted like they’d been hit too.
Mina screamed. “CURTIS!”
Kirishima lunged forward and had to be yanked back by Bakugo. “We can’t just—!”
“You’ll die if you jump in, shitty hair!” Bakugo’s voice cracked. His palms were lit and useless. “Damn it—get up, extra! GET UP!”
Midoriya’s eyes were huge, wet. “No—no, no, no—”
Uraraka had both hands over her mouth, floating uncontrolled. Momo’s created weapon shook in her grip. Todoroki’s ice spread without him noticing. Jiro’s earjacks were pinned flat from the noise. Tsuyu’s grip on Aizawa tightened until her arms trembled.
Aizawa forced himself higher, blood on his teeth, eye wild. “BEARDY! MOVE! DON’T JUST TAKE IT!”
The punches kept falling. Curtis’s arms had come up on instinct, but the armor was failing and Nomu wasn’t slowing. The crater deepened. The class could only watch.
Nomu straightened and stepped out of the crater. Its regeneration had already erased the damage. Yellow eyes stared down at what was left.
Curtis didn’t move. His head was buried in the broken concrete, braids splayed, blood pooling under the shattered mask fragments. The green light on the armor had gone dark. One arm lay half-raised and still. The crater held him like a grave.
The class broke.
Uraraka’s scream tore out of her. Mina dropped to her knees on the walkway. Midoriya staggered forward until Tsuyu’s tongue and Kirishima’s arm stopped him. “Curtis—! CURTIS!”
Bakugo’s explosions died in his palms. His voice came out raw. “No. No, you don’t get to— get up. Get the hell up, you bastard.”
Todoroki’s breath fogged. Ice crawled all the way to his shoulder. Momo’s weapon clattered from her hands. Jiro’s earjacks hung limp. Even Minoru had gone silent, face white.
Aizawa moved.
He shoved Tsuyu’s tongue off with his good arm and forced himself upright. The ruined elbow buckled. He locked it anyway. Capture weapon unspooled, dragging through blood. His eye burned red despite the damage.
“Stay back.” The order came out shredded. He took one step toward the plaza, then another. “All of you. Stay the hell back.”
Nomu’s head turned toward the injured teacher. Aizawa kept walking, scarf rising, ready to erase whatever he could still reach. The final stand started on shaking legs while Curtis lay motionless in the crater behind the monster.
